  "batch_notes": "Inventory gap: properties written as a plain #[test] that calls hegel::Hegel::new(|tc| ...).run() are not in the property lists. rustybuzz's shape_never_panics_on_arbitrary_input (explicitly credited with both rustybuzz bugs) is missing for that reason, and rune's two 'pins' are the same closure style with generators::just, so they are counted as pins although structurally they are Hegel tests. Three 'properties' (rt-format, rust-ini and shell-words doc_examples) take _tc and draw nothing; they are example tests registered as Hegel tests and were classified other. Several rustfft properties compare within a derived floating-point tolerance rather than exactly, which the rubric handles fine but is worth noting for bit-exactness claims. The two rstar duplicate bug records (rstar/1, rstar/2) have empty test fields but are verbatim the KNOWN FAILURE docs of pin properties; several non-pin properties (RTreeModelMachine, hegel_bulk_load_matches_incremental_insertion, rumqtt v4_packet_sequence_roundtrip, salsa DataflowMachine) gate around known bugs in their comments, which is the usual sign that they were the original finders even when the bug record points at the pin. Recurring shape the rubric handles awkwardly: 'limit is honored' properties (rumqtt max packet size in/out, ruzstd window gate) assert both rejection below the limit and acceptance at it; I filed them under rejection. ruzstd's chunked/resumable decode properties compare to the original payload rather than to a one-shot decode, so they sit between internal-consistency and external-oracle; filed as internal-consistency with oracle:external."
